[SPEAKER_15]: Unfortunately, my remote connection isn't working.

152
00:09:10,580 --> 00:09:11,381
[SPEAKER_15]: I'm at home right now.

153
00:09:12,521 --> 00:09:16,804
[SPEAKER_15]: So I don't have the plans to show you right now, but hopefully Todd is going to be able to do that.

154
00:09:16,844 --> 00:09:17,504
[SPEAKER_15]: He's on his way home.

155
00:09:17,969 --> 00:09:19,910
[SPEAKER_15]: he's going to try to log in and email it to me.

156
00:09:20,611 --> 00:09:22,312
[SPEAKER_15]: So I apologize for that.

157
00:09:22,672 --> 00:09:32,239
[SPEAKER_15]: But I kind of wanted to get, since I wasn't able to come to the traffic commission meeting, I didn't hear firsthand one of the comments that I know you guys had.

158
00:09:32,259 --> 00:09:34,000
[SPEAKER_15]: So I wanted to hear it straight from you.

159
00:09:34,700 --> 00:09:44,607
[SPEAKER_15]: Like if you can recall just at the end, basically the termination of the project on the East side, there's like the bus stop and you're approaching to the Felsway.

160
00:09:46,083 --> 00:09:50,345
[SPEAKER_15]: Yeah, I just wanted to hear what you guys, what you thought and what you would prefer, I guess.

161
00:09:51,285 --> 00:09:58,288
[SPEAKER_14]: Should we, if Todd is going to join, I think we did send the plans around to the email list.

162
00:09:58,408 --> 00:10:10,514
[SPEAKER_14]: So I think we have basically had a chance to see it, but do we want to wait on that until Todd is here or we can have the document up on the screen?

163
00:10:12,039 --> 00:10:23,683
[SPEAKER_14]: Um yeah I guess so yeah I mean I mean I can kind of remember what we what was discussed but it would be um or I can run and pull it up for myself.

164
00:10:24,444 --> 00:10:35,888
[SPEAKER_15]: Yeah so I actually I should have said that I do have a um I took a stab at what I think might help um solve the problem that I think you guys were having but again since I didn't hear it straight from you guys I wanted to just

165
00:10:36,866 --> 00:10:39,449
[SPEAKER_15]: Talk through it with you for a second.

166
00:10:40,030 --> 00:10:49,041
[SPEAKER_14]: What I remember was the concern was how the bike lane ends and what the merge area looks like.

167
00:10:50,040 --> 00:11:08,879
[SPEAKER_14]: And that it ends at a, and then the lane markings, there was some confusion because they're drawn onto the plans, but those are not, like the left straight and the right turn lanes, those are not part of the project.

168
00:11:10,621 --> 00:11:17,766
[SPEAKER_14]: So there was some confusion about how it interfaces with those that are not actually going to necessarily be there.

169
00:11:17,786 --> 00:11:26,372
[SPEAKER_14]: I remember one point was when the bike lane basically shares space with the bus stop.

170
00:11:27,012 --> 00:11:34,978
[SPEAKER_14]: I know it's common to paint the bike lane on the right-hand half of the bus stop and the left-hand half of the bus stop becomes a buffer.

171
00:11:36,639 --> 00:11:42,224
[SPEAKER_14]: I think it's preferable to swap those, so put the bike lane painted on the left-hand side of the bus stop.

172
00:11:43,185 --> 00:11:52,373
[SPEAKER_14]: The reason being that shortly after that, the bike lane ends, so cyclists then have to merge into the regular flow of traffic.

173
00:11:53,434 --> 00:11:57,317
[SPEAKER_14]: Not to mention that I always think it looks a little awkward to paint

174
00:11:58,176 --> 00:12:06,281
[SPEAKER_14]: a bike lane on the right-hand side of the bus stop, because then it encourages people to be on the right-hand side of a bus that's going to be letting passengers on and off.

175
00:12:08,382 --> 00:12:17,647
[SPEAKER_14]: And that was one comment, and then also just how that interfaced with the turn lanes after that.

176
00:12:17,807 --> 00:12:21,249
[SPEAKER_14]: But I think there was some confusion about whether those turn lanes are actually really there.

177
00:12:21,269 --> 00:12:26,512
[SPEAKER_15]: Yeah, so because the Bellsway is a DCR road,

178
00:12:27,943 --> 00:12:36,046
[SPEAKER_15]: we would have to go through a process with them in order to strike those lanes that way, or add anything to the approach.

179
00:12:37,186 --> 00:12:42,508
[SPEAKER_15]: And that was, yeah, a mistake not supposed to be included in the package that was sent out.

180
00:12:43,669 --> 00:12:44,629
[SPEAKER_15]: So that was our bad.

181
00:12:44,749 --> 00:12:50,311
[SPEAKER_15]: But as far as the bus stop is concerned, excuse me, maybe it's confusing.

182
00:12:50,871 --> 00:12:52,672
[SPEAKER_15]: So I've been using, I don't know,

183
00:12:53,757 --> 00:12:58,479
[SPEAKER_15]: a standard that I pulled from VTD from the City of Boston Transportation Department.

184
00:12:59,319 --> 00:13:02,380
[SPEAKER_15]: And actually it's not so much putting the bike lane on the right side.

185
00:13:02,420 --> 00:13:09,062
[SPEAKER_15]: It's actually just the bike lane and the bus stop are the same space for that stretch for the length of the bus stop.

186
00:13:09,482 --> 00:13:12,063
[SPEAKER_15]: That's something they've been using and it's one of their standard details.

187
00:13:12,983 --> 00:13:16,364
[SPEAKER_15]: But if it's confusing, like that's obviously it's not our standard.

188
00:13:16,384 --> 00:13:19,465
[SPEAKER_15]: So we don't necessarily have to use it if y'all find it confusing.

189
00:13:20,385 --> 00:13:21,666
[SPEAKER_15]: I think it is a new standard of theirs.

190
00:13:21,706 --> 00:13:22,386
[SPEAKER_15]: So it might be

191
00:13:23,639 --> 00:13:25,065
[SPEAKER_15]: maybe they're getting the same kind of feedback.

192
00:13:25,326 --> 00:13:25,748
[SPEAKER_15]: I'm not sure.

193
00:13:26,460 --> 00:13:31,304
[SPEAKER_14]: Yeah, I've seen this done in a number of places where the bike lane and the bus stop share space.

194
00:13:31,965 --> 00:13:53,303
[SPEAKER_14]: But it always looks like in those places, if the bike lane has been alongside parked cars, like to the left of parked cars, and then the bus stop takes up an area that doesn't have any parked cars, then the bike lane moves against the curb during that area instead of just continuing in a straight line.

195
00:13:54,283 --> 00:14:03,588
[SPEAKER_14]: And that to me encourages, you know, if somebody is just following the bike lane as painted, they're much more likely to be in between that bus and that curb.

196
00:14:04,769 --> 00:14:16,115
[SPEAKER_14]: And there's, and you know, of course you don't have to be, you don't have to do what the paint says, but it just seems like it encourages people to be in the wrong place when a bus is letting off passengers.

197
00:14:18,590 --> 00:14:21,312
[SPEAKER_14]: And it is what they do in other places.

198
00:14:21,713 --> 00:14:24,996
[SPEAKER_14]: And I've seen that before.

199
00:14:25,136 --> 00:14:28,098
[SPEAKER_14]: It just doesn't look like it makes sense to me.

200
00:14:29,540 --> 00:14:32,162
[SPEAKER_14]: Maybe there's another logic to why they do it that way.

201
00:14:32,242 --> 00:14:37,907
[SPEAKER_14]: I suspect it's just because people think, oh, the bike lane needs to always be as far to the right as possible.

202
00:14:37,967 --> 00:14:41,790
[SPEAKER_14]: And if there's room for a buffer between the bike lane and traffic, then it should be there.

203
00:14:42,822 --> 00:14:53,041
[SPEAKER_14]: And that's not a bad way to think about it, except that in this case, I think it just encourages if it encourages people to do anything it encourages people to do the wrong thing.

204
00:14:53,061 --> 00:14:54,584
[SPEAKER_09]: I think there's also.

205
00:14:56,670 --> 00:15:24,717
[SPEAKER_09]: there's a little bit of variation and I'm not quite sure what the standard that we're talking about says in terms of how the bike lane enters the shared stop, because you can have, if the bike lane is like coming up to the bus stop, which is wider than the bike lane, it can either go up to the far right edge of the bus stop, or it can, you know, line up with the left edge.

206
00:15:25,698 --> 00:15:31,303
[SPEAKER_09]: just, you know, not in terms of the markings within the bus stop, but the markings up to the bus stop and after the bus stop.

207
00:15:34,827 --> 00:15:36,408
[SPEAKER_09]: And that can make a difference.

208
00:15:37,029 --> 00:15:40,091
[SPEAKER_14]: Yeah, I think that's kind of what we're saying.

209
00:15:43,957 --> 00:15:44,818
[SPEAKER_14]: Okay, Ernie.

210
00:15:44,878 --> 00:15:45,198
[SPEAKER_09]: Gotcha.

211
00:15:45,639 --> 00:15:45,959
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

212
00:15:46,380 --> 00:15:50,244
[SPEAKER_03]: In addition to the displeasure of being right hooked by a bus.

213
00:15:51,325 --> 00:16:05,640
[SPEAKER_03]: Once we get critical mass of bike riders attempting to pass a bus on the right, it will become an incredible imperilment of discharged passengers, especially elder.

214
00:16:06,447 --> 00:16:13,529
[SPEAKER_03]: elderly or those in physical need to be having to look for bicycles coming as they exit a bus.

215
00:16:13,589 --> 00:16:14,689
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, that's a nightmare.

216
00:16:14,729 --> 00:16:16,089
[SPEAKER_03]: And I agree with Emily there.

217
00:16:16,389 --> 00:16:21,931
[SPEAKER_03]: We're just not seeing it yet because the numbers don't support that kind of hazard awareness.

218
00:16:22,631 --> 00:16:32,133
[SPEAKER_03]: But yeah, we've got to keep the bikes in the left side of the bus and just engineer, if necessary, a successful and slow enough and carefully marked enough

219
00:16:32,633 --> 00:16:37,597
[SPEAKER_03]: scissor intersection so that those lanes can interchange as necessary.

220
00:16:38,138 --> 00:16:46,104
[SPEAKER_14]: Well, I think even if we can't, like if the bus stop overlaps with the bike lane, that's a common thing around here.

221
00:16:46,825 --> 00:16:54,331
[SPEAKER_14]: But the bike lane doesn't need to change direction and go swerve up against the curb in that spot.

222
00:16:54,851 --> 00:16:58,674
[SPEAKER_14]: It can just stay in a straight line, which effectively means that

223
00:16:59,395 --> 00:17:03,838
[SPEAKER_14]: the bike lane aligns with the left edge of the bus stop, not with the right edge.

224
00:17:04,358 --> 00:17:07,560
[SPEAKER_14]: And they do tend to paint it to align with the curb instead.

225
00:17:07,580 --> 00:17:15,044
[SPEAKER_14]: And that's what I think is more likely to indicate to people that they should be on that right edge.

226
00:17:15,084 --> 00:17:18,487
[SPEAKER_14]: And that's more likely that they're going to be in the way of passengers getting on and off.

227
00:17:19,007 --> 00:17:23,590
[SPEAKER_14]: And I don't know why they don't just, you can just paint the bike lane going straight.

228
00:17:24,490 --> 00:17:32,673
[SPEAKER_14]: And like, you know, like it, like Bidan said, this is just about what you do, how it interfaces with that bus stop.

229
00:17:34,474 --> 00:17:40,136
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, we don't want to see signs on buses saying watch out for bicyclists as you exit.

230
00:17:40,536 --> 00:17:46,738
[SPEAKER_14]: I mean, I mean, we're in this case, we're also talking about right after this, the bike lane.

231
00:17:46,918 --> 00:17:50,479
[SPEAKER_06]: I was actually in possess.

