00:55:02,176 --> 00:55:03,556
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just not done yet.

606
00:55:03,997 --> 00:55:07,518
[SPEAKER_04]: In terms of maintenance, yeah, that is MassDOT in some Somerville

607
00:55:08,263 --> 00:55:11,905
[SPEAKER_04]: Some of them agreed to maintain some of the things like flex posts and things like that.

608
00:55:12,566 --> 00:55:16,408
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know who quite where it landed on who is going to street sweep there.

609
00:55:16,989 --> 00:55:27,456
[SPEAKER_11]: Yeah, I just just something that my partner and I have noticed going back and forth the assembly that there's increasing amounts of sand and just debris in those lanes that make it a less than comfortable place to ride.

610
00:55:28,216 --> 00:55:32,900
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if I may diverge for a second because it's related to this area.

611
00:55:35,130 --> 00:55:39,251
[SPEAKER_04]: So there was recently a private development project being proposed at the old Pertucci's.

612
00:55:39,271 --> 00:55:41,392
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know if you all are aware of that.

613
00:55:42,092 --> 00:55:44,872
[SPEAKER_04]: It's a several story laboratory building.

614
00:55:45,252 --> 00:56:01,296
[SPEAKER_04]: So as part of that project, they submitted mitigation requests and told them to recommend it that the board, if it proves the project to require that the proponent at least prepare conceptual plans of a bike lane

615
00:56:02,025 --> 00:56:06,027
[SPEAKER_04]: on Mystic Valley Parkway from Locust to Wellington, both sides.

616
00:56:07,128 --> 00:56:07,468
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, wow.

617
00:56:07,588 --> 00:56:16,473
[SPEAKER_04]: Because right now it's, as most of you know, it's signed as no parking, but yet it's painted as a shoulder, so nobody's using it at all.

618
00:56:16,533 --> 00:56:23,696
[SPEAKER_11]: Nobody except for the occasional, well, the more than occasional police vehicle parked in front of the- The courthouse.

619
00:56:23,736 --> 00:56:24,477
[SPEAKER_11]: The courthouse, yeah.

620
00:56:25,010 --> 00:56:34,977
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, so if that could become buffer bike lanes, much like the ones DCR did on Fellsway, except in this case, there's no parking next to it, that would be great, right?

621
00:56:35,457 --> 00:56:40,721
[SPEAKER_04]: So it would start to connect Locust to these Route 28 bike lanes and other things.

622
00:56:40,761 --> 00:56:43,182
[SPEAKER_04]: It'll start to make all the necessary connections.

623
00:56:44,243 --> 00:56:47,365
[SPEAKER_04]: So we do make comments like that at private developments to the planning board.

624
00:56:47,405 --> 00:56:49,446
[SPEAKER_04]: So hopefully you all see that.

625
00:56:49,547 --> 00:56:51,568
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's how the Locust Street bike lanes came about.

626
00:56:53,090 --> 00:56:53,290
[SPEAKER_11]: Yeah.

627
00:56:53,530 --> 00:57:16,892
[SPEAKER_11]: And I mean, I would say in that area, you have the, uh, McDonald park, the Shady's path on the South side, but that the, if the buffer bike lanes go through, that would be more convenient for accessing other parts of the network as the, the paths kind of had just have you just diving down into Summerville, whether or not you're going there.

628
00:57:17,973 --> 00:57:19,174
[SPEAKER_04]: And the city has had.

629
00:57:19,850 --> 00:57:32,000
[SPEAKER_04]: subsequent meetings with DCR and MassDOT about the underpass that's gonna mirror image the Somerville boardwalk so that that's also progressing, which will be a game changer as a lot of us know, right?

630
00:57:32,020 --> 00:57:34,401
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's the Clippership Connector, correct?

631
00:57:34,882 --> 00:57:35,242
[SPEAKER_11]: No.

632
00:57:35,823 --> 00:57:38,284
[SPEAKER_11]: No, that's the Wellington Greenway.

633
00:57:38,324 --> 00:57:40,826
[SPEAKER_11]: Clippership Connector is the other side of that area.

634
00:57:41,147 --> 00:57:46,010
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, so this is just a Route 28 underpass project is what we refer to it as.

635
00:57:47,271 --> 00:57:47,892
[SPEAKER_14]: Can we go to Pat?

636
00:57:49,112 --> 00:57:58,078
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I just like to, I was going to comment on the neighborhoods where people want to go, but like Route 16, I ride on it all the time.

637
00:57:58,738 --> 00:58:03,661
[SPEAKER_02]: And that side is really important for people who are doing shopping and things.

638
00:58:03,721 --> 00:58:09,505
[SPEAKER_02]: So like when I'm going home from the CVS or the paint store or Aldi, I always ride there.

639
00:58:09,525 --> 00:58:10,626
[SPEAKER_02]: So it'd be terrific.

640
00:58:11,606 --> 00:58:33,778
[SPEAKER_02]: um but I think in terms of like where people go I think like the routes that are on the um the master plan are really you know those are that's a good indication of the places people want to go and um I can you know that sort of tells you where they want to go but there are two that I can tell you people do not want to ride on.

641
00:58:34,578 --> 00:58:42,300
[SPEAKER_02]: One is Main Street, so like that section that like kind of between George and up to Sparks Park.

642
00:58:43,660 --> 00:58:54,123
[SPEAKER_02]: And I think like there are a lot of kind of neighborhood alternate routes, right of thing that kind of parallel Main Street.

643
00:58:54,803 --> 00:59:01,125
[SPEAKER_02]: So I think that like would be the neighborhood concept would be helpful in that neighborhood.

644
00:59:02,405 --> 00:59:15,632
[SPEAKER_02]: And also just like the pavement condition on Main Street is just it's been terrible for years and it is really like quite dangerous to ride a bike on or a motorcycle or a car.

645
00:59:17,313 --> 00:59:20,734
[SPEAKER_02]: And then the other one that like I hear a lot about is Riverside Ave.

646
00:59:21,234 --> 00:59:26,195
[SPEAKER_02]: And I think, yeah, the Clippership connector will provide an alternative to that.

647
00:59:26,515 --> 00:59:31,316
[SPEAKER_02]: And we have discussed plans for that previously.

648
00:59:31,336 --> 00:59:36,897
[SPEAKER_02]: So I think that one does, Riverside comes up a lot in conversations with people.

649
00:59:37,917 --> 00:59:40,338
[SPEAKER_02]: That one I feel probably should be a priority.

650
00:59:42,738 --> 00:59:55,829
[SPEAKER_14]: And I think it is also worth pointing out that Main Street has a lot of businesses that theoretically should be appealing to bicyclists passing by and bicyclists will not stop at any of those places if they don't go down that street at all.

651
00:59:57,250 --> 01:00:00,433
[SPEAKER_14]: So that is maybe something to keep in mind in a lot of these places.

652
01:00:00,453 --> 01:00:01,073
[SPEAKER_14]: B. Dan?

653
01:00:01,814 --> 01:00:02,054
[SPEAKER_09]: Yeah.

654
01:00:04,132 --> 01:00:07,075
[SPEAKER_09]: So I'm happy to submit all of my routes.

655
01:00:07,175 --> 01:00:14,421
[SPEAKER_09]: I'm not sure what the best way to do that, maybe Google Maps route instructions, that sort of thing.

656
01:00:14,441 --> 01:00:20,767
[SPEAKER_15]: Just draw them on an aerial, take a little snap screenshot.

657
01:00:21,187 --> 01:00:22,928
[SPEAKER_15]: Doesn't have to be anything crazy.

658
01:00:26,211 --> 01:00:30,792
[SPEAKER_09]: The other thing is, I love the idea of taking more data.

659
01:00:30,812 --> 01:00:44,876
[SPEAKER_09]: Something I brought up before, on occasion, is the idea of adopting standards that would make use of data, so that we can say, okay, if it's this wide, this is what we should do.

660
01:00:47,116 --> 01:00:53,842
[SPEAKER_09]: all things being equal so that we're not having to debate every single street individually.

661
01:00:53,862 --> 01:01:04,612
[SPEAKER_09]: And we can just say, OK, these are our standards for a street of this width and this level of traffic and that sort of thing.

662
01:01:04,652 --> 01:01:09,596
[SPEAKER_09]: And there are a few sets of standards like that that other places use.

663
01:01:09,636 --> 01:01:13,339
[SPEAKER_09]: I think we should not make our own street, just adopt one of those.

664
01:01:15,237 --> 01:01:25,736
[SPEAKER_09]: But we should seriously try to adopt one of those existing sets of standards so that we don't have to think about it quite so hard for every individual street.

665
01:01:26,955 --> 01:01:49,630
[SPEAKER_14]: I wonder, correct me if I'm wrong, but I sort of get the impression from the specific places we've talked about, when it comes to the choice between a buffered bike lane or just a regular bike lane or a separated facility, we're always so, so limited by the width of the street.

666
01:01:51,447 --> 01:01:59,919
[SPEAKER_14]: we just don't really have enough choices for that to really come up.

667
01:02:00,039 --> 01:02:01,461
[SPEAKER_14]: I mean, I agree with you.

668
01:02:04,951 --> 01:02:11,393
[SPEAKER_09]: It's not that we necessarily have choices, but every time we have to figure out what is the one thing that fits in here.

669
01:02:11,413 --> 01:02:17,976
[SPEAKER_09]: And we're like talking about, okay, well, you know, can we do this thing given how wide this is?

670
01:02:17,996 --> 01:02:29,040
[SPEAKER_09]: And every time we're talking about specific widths, whereas if we had a standard set of standards that said, this is what you can do in this width, you wouldn't have to talk about it every time.

671
01:02:29,060 --> 01:02:32,041
[SPEAKER_09]: You say, this is what the standard says for this width.

672
01:02:37,918 --> 01:02:41,122
[SPEAKER_14]: Todd or Amy, do you want to add to that or should we go on to the next?

673
01:02:41,142 --> 01:02:44,346
[SPEAKER_04]: I just had a question.

674
01:02:44,547 --> 01:02:45,007
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, sorry.

675
01:02:45,087 --> 01:02:46,729
[SPEAKER_04]: Go ahead, Amy.

676
01:02:47,070 --> 01:02:55,801
[SPEAKER_15]: It is the idea of coming up with various standards is something that I've definitely been thinking about, something I've done in the past at other places.

677
01:02:56,962 --> 01:03:11,501
[SPEAKER_15]: But I do think it's a good idea what exactly what types of standards those are whether that's like a prescription for a cross section for a type of street or more just like what does a buffered bike lane look like, or a lot of other different things.

678
01:03:13,383 --> 01:03:14,404
[SPEAKER_15]: think about a little bit.

679
01:03:15,385 --> 01:03:18,246
[SPEAKER_15]: To Emily's point, like most of our roads are pretty narrow.

680
01:03:19,387 --> 01:03:23,210
[SPEAKER_15]: So often we don't have a ton of options, unfortunately, but we do have options.

681
01:03:24,530 --> 01:03:26,452
[SPEAKER_15]: And I think it's I think it would be helpful.

682
01:03:26,472 --> 01:03:38,519
[SPEAKER_15]: Oh, yeah, similar to like our, our bus stop conversation early earlier Salem Street, like I was using a BTD standard.

683
01:03:39,120 --> 01:03:39,340
[SPEAKER_15]: If we

684
01:03:39,940 --> 01:03:46,562
[SPEAKER_15]: can create some of our own standards for situations like that that happen all over the place, then I think that that would be really helpful.

685
01:03:47,002 --> 01:03:49,443
[SPEAKER_15]: And then we can just plop them in.

686
01:03:50,263 --> 01:03:57,265
[SPEAKER_15]: And obviously there'll be other situations that arise that we don't have a standard for that are just kind of unique.

687
01:03:57,285 --> 01:03:59,706
[SPEAKER_15]: These things happen, but it's a starting place.

688
01:03:59,786 --> 01:04:03,667
[SPEAKER_15]: And I think, yeah, generally that's something that I would like to do for the city.

689
01:04:04,314 --> 01:04:16,220
[SPEAKER_14]: So is that something that we should talk about putting together a working group to work with you on, or is that something that's on the line, or that's something that can be part of the master plan project?

690
01:04:18,041 --> 01:04:18,662
[SPEAKER_15]: Possibly.

691
01:04:20,983 --> 01:04:23,504
[SPEAKER_15]: Possibly is the answer to all those questions.

692
01:04:24,325 --> 01:04:28,467
[SPEAKER_15]: I think I was thinking about also it being maybe part of the master plan.

693
01:04:28,487 --> 01:04:28,987
[SPEAKER_15]: I'm not 100% sure.

694
01:04:29,007 --> 01:04:30,528
[SPEAKER_15]: We'd have to talk with Todd,

695
01:04:32,273 --> 01:04:42,539
[SPEAKER_15]: trying to figure that out, but I think it's, yeah, it's an interesting idea, but I definitely would like to work with you guys on it, on creating it for sure, and get your continuous feedback and stuff.

696
01:04:43,620 --> 01:04:43,960
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah.

697
01:04:44,080 --> 01:04:49,623
[SPEAKER_14]: Maybe add that to the next meeting agenda as something that we want to figure out what our next steps are.

698
01:04:49,643 --> 01:04:52,085
[SPEAKER_14]: Yeah.

699
01:04:55,487 --> 01:05:01,931
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, even something that came up at the Salem Street Traffic Commission, and this does relate to standards in the sense that

700
01:05:03,383 --> 01:05:09,821
[SPEAKER_04]: Some folks on the call I don't think they were meant for bicycle commission members but maybe they were.

701
01:05:11,256 --> 01:05:17,539
[SPEAKER_04]: Some pointed out the state's policy about buffered bike lanes only, especially if it's speed or volume or such and such, right?

702
01:05:18,019 --> 01:05:20,040
[SPEAKER_04]: But in Medford, we don't have a lot of that luxury.

703
01:05:20,100 --> 01:05:31,485
