01:19:54,137 --> 01:19:55,418
[SPEAKER_15]: Think about those situations and

836
01:19:56,090 --> 01:19:58,093
[SPEAKER_15]: and let me know what they are as well.

837
01:19:58,213 --> 01:19:59,094
[SPEAKER_15]: That would be really helpful.

838
01:19:59,614 --> 01:20:02,318
[SPEAKER_14]: This could be a good thing to ask on the social medias.

839
01:20:02,338 --> 01:20:03,719
[SPEAKER_14]: Yeah.

840
01:20:04,480 --> 01:20:04,841
[SPEAKER_15]: Good call.

841
01:20:08,245 --> 01:20:08,425
[SPEAKER_15]: Todd.

842
01:20:10,380 --> 01:20:17,925
[SPEAKER_04]: I just had another quick update that's relative to bicycling, but it doesn't necessarily always get the same press as bike lanes.

843
01:20:18,545 --> 01:20:29,833
[SPEAKER_04]: A while back, I went out with Bruce Kulik to check on roadway loops, whether they could sense a bicyclist or not on Harvard Ave at Boston Ave.

844
01:20:30,493 --> 01:20:32,995
[SPEAKER_04]: And there were certain spots that could and certain spots that couldn't.

845
01:20:33,455 --> 01:20:36,637
[SPEAKER_04]: So the city, Amy and I, good work, Amy,

846
01:20:37,660 --> 01:20:40,581
[SPEAKER_04]: applied for a bottleneck grant from MassDOT and we received it.

847
01:20:41,101 --> 01:20:50,423
[SPEAKER_04]: So that grant involves putting video detection on all the bus nav signalizing sections.

848
01:20:50,963 --> 01:20:55,944
[SPEAKER_04]: And video detection will be much better at sensing cyclists than the old roadway loops.

849
01:20:56,124 --> 01:20:59,884
[SPEAKER_04]: And we won't have to worry about utility companies digging them up.

850
01:21:00,584 --> 01:21:02,765
[SPEAKER_04]: So eventually, it's happening this summer.

851
01:21:03,702 --> 01:21:06,703
[SPEAKER_04]: All the intersections besides Winthrop Street will have them.

852
01:21:06,723 --> 01:21:10,664
[SPEAKER_04]: And then once we rebuild Winthrop Street at Boston Ave entirely, that will have it as well.

853
01:21:11,484 --> 01:21:15,965
[SPEAKER_04]: So just a little side note, because we don't get to talk about all this stuff usually.

854
01:21:16,726 --> 01:21:18,166
[SPEAKER_14]: Do those still work in the dark?

855
01:21:19,746 --> 01:21:21,187
[SPEAKER_04]: The technology is getting better and better.

856
01:21:21,207 --> 01:21:23,868
[SPEAKER_04]: It used to have problems with snow and birds and things like that.

857
01:21:23,928 --> 01:21:27,789
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's supposedly all good now.

858
01:21:28,029 --> 01:21:32,610
[SPEAKER_04]: So it's MassDOT-approved equipment, so I'm confident.

859
01:21:33,485 --> 01:21:45,310
[SPEAKER_05]: One thing I'd like to point out about those is there's an intersection out in Lexington that uses that technology and the cameras are not aimed at the location where they request that bike stop.

860
01:21:46,011 --> 01:21:48,632
[SPEAKER_05]: It's actually aimed 20 feet behind that.

861
01:21:49,212 --> 01:21:57,216
[SPEAKER_05]: So if you roll up and wait on the bike symbol at behind the stop line, it will never detect you.

862
01:21:57,236 --> 01:22:00,617
[SPEAKER_05]: Geez, but this is Merritt Road out in Lexington.

863
01:22:00,997 --> 01:22:02,258
[SPEAKER_05]: So I just want to throw out that

864
01:22:03,249 --> 01:22:20,239
[SPEAKER_05]: If they use that tech, they should be pretty sure to have some sort of painting on the ground where cyclists can expect to wait and they will be within view because it's a very frustrating experience to have it aimed at a location other than that.

865
01:22:20,259 --> 01:22:22,220
[SPEAKER_04]: That's a great point.

866
01:22:22,400 --> 01:22:25,062
[SPEAKER_04]: We can work with you to troubleshoot that too after they're in.

867
01:22:25,627 --> 01:22:30,670
[SPEAKER_04]: These ones are even the next tech where it's just one video camera for the entire section.

868
01:22:31,410 --> 01:22:34,652
[SPEAKER_04]: So the original ones had one per approach and it's directed.

869
01:22:35,072 --> 01:22:36,813
[SPEAKER_04]: These new ones are one per inch section.

870
01:22:36,833 --> 01:22:38,134
[SPEAKER_04]: It sees everything all at once.

871
01:22:39,454 --> 01:22:43,577
[SPEAKER_04]: So, but it's still gonna have a detection zone like you're saying, Daniel.

872
01:22:43,617 --> 01:22:48,820
[SPEAKER_04]: So, but you know, for Medford, a lot of our locations didn't have any sensors at all.

873
01:22:48,860 --> 01:22:50,380
[SPEAKER_04]: So no matter where you were stopping.

874
01:22:52,001 --> 01:22:52,962
[SPEAKER_04]: So hopefully this is better.

875
01:22:59,758 --> 01:23:01,380
[SPEAKER_14]: Should we go on to winter street.

876
01:23:09,109 --> 01:23:10,250
[SPEAKER_15]: That you want to start on that one.

877
01:23:13,214 --> 01:23:13,494
[SPEAKER_04]: Sure.

878
01:23:17,518 --> 01:23:22,621
[SPEAKER_04]: The plan is, again, to have bicycle infrastructure from South Street all the way to the Winchester line.

879
01:23:24,222 --> 01:23:31,086
[SPEAKER_04]: So for the most part, Amy, correct me if I'm wrong, that includes a bike lane on each side of the street.

880
01:23:31,246 --> 01:23:38,150
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't think there are any segments that would have, if there are, there'd be a very short segment of just a share.

881
01:23:38,550 --> 01:23:46,315
[SPEAKER_04]: But the plan is to have bike lane each direction, whether that's buffered or not, it depends on the option.

882
01:23:47,167 --> 01:24:05,799
[SPEAKER_04]: that goes forward if we remove all parking with the exception of, you know, certain blocks that have indents for them, then we'd be able to do buffered everywhere but if we only remove, say one side then it might be more like the five to six foot bike lane each side.

883
01:24:06,199 --> 01:24:10,782
[SPEAKER_04]: But the idea is to get you all the way from South Street to Winchester line.

884
01:24:11,082 --> 01:24:14,925
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it did already exist from the high school north,

885
01:24:16,023 --> 01:24:20,627
[SPEAKER_04]: They just weren't painted very well the last few years and then Eversource has been out there for four years.

886
01:24:22,148 --> 01:24:34,438
[SPEAKER_04]: It's challenging but so yeah Eversource got to complete their work, and then they're on the hook to restore curb to curb so they're going to repave just like they did sell in Winthrop.

887
01:24:35,339 --> 01:24:37,160
[SPEAKER_04]: So the repaving for Winthrop Street.

888
01:24:38,338 --> 01:24:41,061
[SPEAKER_04]: is not all the way from South, it's from Route 16.

889
01:24:41,161 --> 01:24:50,469
[SPEAKER_04]: So the one block between 16 and South is not going to be repaved as part of what Eversource, but from 16 all the way up to the Winchester line, it will eventually be repaved.

890
01:24:50,930 --> 01:25:02,000
[SPEAKER_04]: Schedule wise is just to be determined, because as most people know, they're still doing work in some areas and they finish the bulk of the work in other areas with temporary patches, right?

891
01:25:04,309 --> 01:25:16,037
[SPEAKER_04]: So that's in terms of, yeah, in terms of priority, we need to give Eversource a restoration plan whenever that restoration is going to occur.

892
01:25:16,978 --> 01:25:20,501
[SPEAKER_04]: And also we hope to meet with the community about it as well, so.

893
01:25:22,202 --> 01:25:24,644
[SPEAKER_07]: So question on the Winthrop Street stuff.

894
01:25:25,104 --> 01:25:29,928
[SPEAKER_07]: How's that interacting with the new development that's going in next to the high school?

895
01:25:32,022 --> 01:25:37,724
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, depending on the timing of it, either they have to match us or, you know, basically.

896
01:25:38,744 --> 01:25:39,685
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, it's a good point.

897
01:25:39,785 --> 01:25:42,066
[SPEAKER_04]: So, any project South Street we're dealing with that.

898
01:25:44,106 --> 01:25:50,048
[SPEAKER_04]: If the bike lanes are already there, that will become the new existing conditions and they have to, you know, work around that or vice versa.

899
01:25:50,069 --> 01:25:53,870
[SPEAKER_04]: There's a crosswalk going in with that new development that's not there right now.

900
01:25:55,049 --> 01:26:01,991
[SPEAKER_04]: It just depends on whoever goes in last will have to accommodate it and whatever's there at that moment will be the new existing condition.

901
01:26:02,131 --> 01:26:07,733
[SPEAKER_07]: So yeah, it's mostly, you know, just thinking about, you know, go through, do all the work.

902
01:26:08,293 --> 01:26:14,775
[SPEAKER_07]: And then if they're still, you know, running their heavy equipment, they're gonna just trash it all.

903
01:26:16,761 --> 01:26:18,102
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, we hope that's not the case.

904
01:26:18,122 --> 01:26:33,572
[SPEAKER_04]: But there's a moratorium when when a street gets paved for five years, you have to do more than the standard trench, you have to do much larger areas so that avoids the deterioration of the pavement so soon after doing it so.

905
01:26:36,013 --> 01:26:36,834
[SPEAKER_14]: Matt, you have your hand up.

906
01:26:40,043 --> 01:26:45,848
[SPEAKER_10]: One thing I wanted to flag on the Winthrop Street, and this kind of goes back to what Ernie brought up earlier about parking.

907
01:26:46,708 --> 01:26:55,094
[SPEAKER_10]: You wouldn't really think a lot of people park on Winthrop Street, but the American Legion sometimes has the events, and then the road is just like flooded.

908
01:26:56,515 --> 01:27:01,399
[SPEAKER_10]: And I could imagine them being vehemently opposed to, and some of the neighbors perhaps too.

909
01:27:01,419 --> 01:27:04,381
[SPEAKER_10]: There's more parking on the lower part of Winthrop than you might anticipate.

910
01:27:04,741 --> 01:27:06,503
[SPEAKER_10]: So obviously Todd and Amy can't,

911
01:27:07,343 --> 01:27:08,424
[SPEAKER_10]: have a crystal ball, like you said.

912
01:27:09,064 --> 01:27:20,953
[SPEAKER_10]: But we don't, I don't think we want to be in a situation like Cambridge, where, you know, you have just cast on it, think share the article, you know, just like all they feel people feel like this is sprung upon them, and you know, you're stealing their parking.

913
01:27:21,353 --> 01:27:24,316
[SPEAKER_10]: And so it might help just to talk to the Legion in advance.

914
01:27:24,336 --> 01:27:26,257
[SPEAKER_10]: That's the main I live in that area.

915
01:27:26,557 --> 01:27:29,539
[SPEAKER_10]: That's the main parking issue I see there.

916
01:27:29,619 --> 01:27:31,701
[SPEAKER_10]: Otherwise, I wouldn't expect a lot of pushback, but you never know.

917
01:27:33,438 --> 01:27:33,819
[SPEAKER_15]: Good point.

918
01:27:34,440 --> 01:27:37,384
[SPEAKER_15]: We've been collecting a bunch of parking data at various times.

919
01:27:38,907 --> 01:27:44,676
[SPEAKER_15]: Some strategic, some of those have been strategic times like over by Victory Park, when there's like little league games and stuff like that.

920
01:27:45,397 --> 01:27:47,220
[SPEAKER_15]: And also Memorial Park I think it's called.

921
01:27:48,223 --> 01:27:50,845
[SPEAKER_15]: We're on the south edge of the project.

922
01:27:51,545 --> 01:27:55,487
[SPEAKER_15]: Um, so that way we have the data to, you know, when people say, Oh, what about the baseball games?

923
01:27:55,527 --> 01:27:56,508
[SPEAKER_15]: We're like, Oh, here you go.

924
01:27:57,248 --> 01:27:58,769
[SPEAKER_15]: Here's the, here's our parking data.

925
01:27:58,809 --> 01:28:02,872
[SPEAKER_15]: So we can certainly park, uh, collect some parking data around there as well.

926
01:28:03,612 --> 01:28:03,812
[SPEAKER_10]: Yeah.

927
01:28:03,852 --> 01:28:06,914
[SPEAKER_10]: Cause I'm not sure if that would really come up in your data.

928
01:28:06,954 --> 01:28:07,595
[SPEAKER_10]: I hope it would.

929
01:28:07,795 --> 01:28:16,500
[SPEAKER_10]: Cause it's like maybe once or twice a month for the most part, it doesn't seem like a problem, but they must have events every once in a while that it's just a ton of parking.

930
01:28:17,113 --> 01:28:18,914
[SPEAKER_10]: Yeah, maybe they have space for their own.

931
01:28:19,634 --> 01:28:24,115
[SPEAKER_10]: I mean, they have some law, maybe they need to expand their private law, but I don't know.

932
01:28:24,576 --> 01:28:32,318
[SPEAKER_04]: Thank you for flagging that because as Amy just said, we've tried to be strategic to capture those events so that we could say, oh, we looked at that, you know?

933
01:28:33,839 --> 01:28:38,220
[SPEAKER_04]: So we'll have to peruse their calendar to see when their next event is.

934
01:28:40,261 --> 01:28:45,843
[SPEAKER_15]: I do have, sorry, I do have two versions of the plan so far.

935
01:28:47,268 --> 01:28:59,996
[SPEAKER_15]: that can be one that's like taking away almost all of the parking, except for over near Victory Park, I believe, and maybe one other little spot, and one that's preserving a lot of it, but also not all of it.

936
01:29:00,757 --> 01:29:08,722
[SPEAKER_15]: And so we can certainly like, you know, mix and match pieces as we need to, depending on what the data tells us and what the public

937
01:29:09,378 --> 01:29:10,899
[SPEAKER_15]: you know, hates or whatever.

938
01:29:13,081 --> 01:29:27,353
[SPEAKER_14]: I think since this is also going by the high school, I think this would be a great opportunity to involve the high school students who either bike to school now or are interested in biking to school.

939
01:29:28,193 --> 01:29:38,502
[SPEAKER_14]: One, you know, we've, I think we've tossed around a little bit before everybody has their own ideas about whether you like or don't like various types of buffered

940
01:29:40,404 --> 01:29:55,961
[SPEAKER_14]: One concern that I have with some of the types of buffered bike lanes that you see around the area is that the bike lane gets plowed by its special plow and the road gets plowed and the area in between with the bollards

941
01:29:56,722 --> 01:30:00,946
[SPEAKER_14]: collects a mountain of snow and then there's also a mountain of snow on the edge of the sidewalk.

942
01:30:01,487 --> 01:30:11,877
[SPEAKER_14]: So then it melts and it runs across the bike lane because of the crown of the road and then there's a sheet of ice across the bike lane and that condition will last

943
01:30:12,618 --> 01:30:16,420
[SPEAKER_14]: for a pretty long time and it kind of comes and comes back every morning.

944
01:30:16,440 --> 01:30:22,564
[SPEAKER_14]: And that keeps going for a long time after the rest of the roads everywhere else are completely dry.

945
01:30:22,584 --> 01:30:32,910
[SPEAKER_14]: And so that means that if you're looking out your window of your house to see is the road conditions good enough to bike to school today, it might look like it's perfectly good.

946
01:30:32,950 --> 01:30:34,551
[SPEAKER_14]: And in most places it's perfectly good.

947
01:30:34,611 --> 01:30:41,595
[SPEAKER_14]: And then you get to the one spot where you have this nice buffered bike lane and it's either it's a sheet of ice or it has intermittent ice.

948
01:30:42,915 --> 01:30:46,637
[SPEAKER_14]: And that's most likely to be a problem in the morning when kids are going to school.

949
01:30:47,758 --> 01:30:56,322
[SPEAKER_14]: And that's and I think that's something that we just want to keep in mind again, because we want this to be safe for kids to use on the way to school.

950
01:30:56,362 --> 01:31:05,327
[SPEAKER_14]: And it's the kind of thing that you won't be able to tell necessarily just by looking out at what the condition of your own street is, because it's kind of a very specific.

951
01:31:05,527 --> 01:31:11,270
[SPEAKER_14]: It's very specific to certain certain types of buffered bike lanes and.

952
01:31:12,310 --> 01:31:14,891
[SPEAKER_14]: I think there are probably ways to avoid it.

953
01:31:15,131 --> 01:31:21,653
[SPEAKER_14]: And if we end up with a buffered bike lane, there are some types of them that are more prone to this problem than others.

954
01:31:22,614 --> 01:31:28,135
[SPEAKER_14]: But it's something that we should just keep in mind as a consideration.

955
01:31:28,756 --> 01:31:30,656
[SPEAKER_14]: For a lot of the year, it's not a problem.

956
01:31:30,716 --> 01:31:34,057
[SPEAKER_14]: But then it could actually be a problem for a big chunk of the year.

957
01:31:35,947 --> 01:31:43,313
[SPEAKER_14]: and it could make those bike lanes unusable at times when the rest of the road is otherwise in perfectly good shape.

958
01:31:44,815 --> 01:31:49,219
[SPEAKER_14]: So whatever we do, I think we need to make sure that that's taken into account too.

959
01:31:50,019 --> 01:31:57,025
[SPEAKER_04]: Emily, that would be valuable information for us because maintenance-wise, it's a challenge to do any of this sometimes, right?

960
01:31:57,105 --> 01:32:02,090
[SPEAKER_04]: So knowing if you all felt strongly about that and you thought,

961
01:32:03,042 --> 01:32:17,626
[SPEAKER_04]: it was better just to have a painted buffer versus painted with FlexPost because of the reason you just described, then we would know to, you know, focus our efforts on things other than the FlexPost for the buffer pipeline.

962
01:32:18,046 --> 01:32:22,387
[SPEAKER_14]: And that may be something that we need input from parents and students, too.

963
01:32:23,368 --> 01:32:31,930
[SPEAKER_14]: If this is, you know, of all places, this really, this is something we really need to hear from those people, I think.

964
01:32:32,523 --> 01:32:33,944
[SPEAKER_12]: Yeah, I agree with that, though.

965
01:32:33,964 --> 01:32:38,708
[SPEAKER_12]: I think that the majority of the year flux posts and a buffered bike lane will help.

966
01:32:38,848 --> 01:32:54,801
[SPEAKER_12]: And my experience in other biking and other, um, buffered bike lanes, even in the kind of the best conditions where they've plowed and everything, there is that ice issue, but at the same time, it rarely lasts more than, you know, a certain period of time.

967
01:32:54,861 --> 01:32:58,805
[SPEAKER_12]: And I still think that it is valuable to have that, the flux puts on buffers.

968
01:32:59,452 --> 01:33:01,794
[SPEAKER_12]: when possible, especially for a school.

969
01:33:02,934 --> 01:33:17,844
[SPEAKER_12]: So even if there's a couple of days after a storm it takes to get cleared and people can't bike, there's still like 360 days they could use it or 350, it only takes out a couple of days of bad weather.

970
01:33:17,864 --> 01:33:22,427
[SPEAKER_12]: So I think I support protected bike lanes whenever possible.

971
01:33:27,779 --> 01:33:31,761
[SPEAKER_10]: Can I ask what the plan is for the rotary at High Street in Winthrop?

972
01:33:32,262 --> 01:33:37,845
[SPEAKER_10]: Is that going to be nice like Powder House or probably something less of a build than that?

973
01:33:44,330 --> 01:33:49,852
[SPEAKER_04]: At this time, it will probably be more like a traditional markings towards traditional circle around the boat.

974
01:33:50,992 --> 01:33:53,613
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know if we have the same amount of room they had in powerhouse.

975
01:33:53,693 --> 01:33:55,433
[SPEAKER_04]: Powerhouse had a lot of extra pavement.

976
01:33:56,053 --> 01:33:56,713
[SPEAKER_04]: Sure.

977
01:33:57,153 --> 01:34:01,494
[SPEAKER_04]: But yeah, it would be nice to have some sort of hybrid getting closer to that.

978
01:34:03,315 --> 01:34:05,115
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, Amy and I will keep looking at it.

979
01:34:05,135 --> 01:34:07,416
[SPEAKER_04]: It's a tough spot.

980
01:34:07,456 --> 01:34:08,536
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, I don't really know.

981
01:34:10,637 --> 01:34:11,497
[SPEAKER_15]: Yeah, I do have some.

982
01:34:13,351 --> 01:34:27,460
[SPEAKER_15]: Currently it's just a shared condition for bikes going through that roundabout, but I do have some additional kind of tightening up and closing off of slip lanes that I'm proposing as well.

983
01:34:28,481 --> 01:34:36,306
[SPEAKER_15]: I think will help keep the cars going in one direction, not flying all over the place, which is what I think makes that particularly dangerous.

984
01:34:40,909 --> 01:34:48,614
[SPEAKER_14]: If this, I think it sounds like we're up to date on Winthrop Street as it currently stands.

985
01:34:50,975 --> 01:34:56,679
[SPEAKER_14]: Is there other, is there anything else on this topic before we go on?

986
01:35:03,863 --> 01:35:10,026
[SPEAKER_14]: In that case, we have upcoming events and the Bike Lights for Kids project.

987
01:35:11,347 --> 01:35:13,748
[SPEAKER_14]: Those are kind of, those two things kind of tie together.

988
01:35:13,768 --> 01:35:18,830
[SPEAKER_14]: Ernie, do you want to give an update on the Bike Lights for Kids?

989
01:35:19,410 --> 01:35:20,371
[SPEAKER_03]: Sure.

990
01:35:20,991 --> 01:35:21,792
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm not muted.

991
01:35:21,892 --> 01:35:22,172
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay.

992
01:35:23,372 --> 01:35:28,895
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, I sent some information in the interim, just so as to not take so much time in our

993
01:35:29,485 --> 01:35:51,370
[SPEAKER_03]: busy schedule of the 240 lights kits that arrived whenever it was a few weeks ago, I've distributed 200 of them and have about, I'll have a good three dozen or so for our June 3rd rodeo at Tufts Park.

994
01:35:52,450 --> 01:35:59,292
[SPEAKER_03]: There should be, I think the second batch of 240 left Singapore

995
01:36:00,277 --> 01:36:03,758
[SPEAKER_03]: around May 10th and take a month to get here.

996
01:36:04,258 --> 01:36:08,039
[SPEAKER_03]: So they're not going to arrive certainly by next week.

997
01:36:10,059 --> 01:36:22,662
[SPEAKER_03]: I have everything needed for that, supplies for that shipment, except for the league of cyclists bike guide.

998
01:36:23,122 --> 01:36:25,403
[SPEAKER_03]: So in the interest of time, I'll order

999
01:36:26,639 --> 01:36:33,467
[SPEAKER_03]: 250 of those on my own pay for them so as to get them in time for distribution.

1000
01:36:33,487 --> 01:36:34,087
[SPEAKER_03]: My hope.

1001
01:36:34,915 --> 01:36:57,529
[SPEAKER_03]: in talking to the various vice and principals of the schools is to visit them again before June 23rd and give them what they think is a reasonable supply to distribute at recess or little events or talks I present there or whatever to be able to feed some of the population for the summer.

1002
01:36:58,210 --> 01:37:02,453
[SPEAKER_03]: So I'm hoping that there'll be a big distribution of the second lot

1003
01:37:03,520 --> 01:37:06,142
[SPEAKER_03]: shipment of them in mid June.

1004
01:37:06,162 --> 01:37:17,068
[SPEAKER_03]: I may have secured a sponsor for a third shipment for September already, but I'm hasn't committed to it yet.

1005
01:37:17,188 --> 01:37:19,810
[SPEAKER_03]: But that's really helpful.

1006
01:37:22,532 --> 01:37:24,373
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, just as an aside.

1007
01:37:24,393 --> 01:37:25,413
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, yeah.

1008
01:37:25,473 --> 01:37:27,755
[SPEAKER_03]: So Pat, did you want to talk about June 3?

1009
01:37:28,853 --> 01:37:36,616
[SPEAKER_03]: And I was just thinking that if we are going to do that, would it be that we're going to have that event?

1010
01:37:37,056 --> 01:37:54,183
[SPEAKER_03]: Could we have a big map there and try to combine some tasks by asking parents who show up what they think are hot spots in the city that we might prioritize or work on and let them mark up a map

1011
01:37:54,886 --> 01:37:58,689
[SPEAKER_03]: And we'll provide that to Todd and Amy as well as our own comments.

1012
01:37:59,410 --> 01:38:01,631
[SPEAKER_03]: Just seems like maybe it would kill time.

1013
01:38:01,651 --> 01:38:06,795
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I think that's a good idea, because that will be a good opportunity to engage grownups.

1014
01:38:07,796 --> 01:38:16,002
[SPEAKER_02]: I think something more formal, I'd be hesitant to add that aspect to it, since we've got a lot going on already.

1015
01:38:17,083 --> 01:38:21,824
[SPEAKER_02]: You certainly want to bring this with us and put it on display.

1016
01:38:21,844 --> 01:38:31,086
[SPEAKER_03]: A map and some magic markers and ask parents to, I don't know, mark where they live, where they like to cycle, where they see a problem intersections, you know, just.

1017
01:38:31,287 --> 01:38:38,528
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I think what I would do is keep more of a like a conversation rather than having them like write something out.

1018
01:38:38,548 --> 01:38:40,789
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that's going to be hard to manage.

1019
01:38:41,369 --> 01:38:45,270
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, just have them mark one map that you have a large map

1020
01:38:45,592 --> 01:38:49,914
[SPEAKER_03]: We have a large map posted on a table next to the helmets and lights and all that.

1021
01:38:49,954 --> 01:39:01,919
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but that's I think logistically, I think that's going to be difficult, like like producing a large map and even like the space for like directing that.

1022
01:39:02,759 --> 01:39:07,101
[SPEAKER_02]: But I think I think like engaging people and asking would be really good.

1023
01:39:09,342 --> 01:39:15,284
[SPEAKER_03]: Someone asked, I posted before this photo of this darling kid, Clementine.

1024
01:39:15,912 --> 01:39:20,156
[SPEAKER_03]: all well suited up with the first lights kit on her bike.

1025
01:39:20,757 --> 01:39:34,109
[SPEAKER_03]: And again, her parent who is, she's a student, I think at the Roberts here, parent gave us permission to use that on the city websites or any promotional materials on our behalf.

1026
01:39:34,909 --> 01:39:36,791
[SPEAKER_03]: But I'm not gonna take the lead on that.

1027
01:39:36,851 --> 01:39:41,696
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know where that photo is, but one of you must have it in their archives.

1028
01:39:43,582 --> 01:39:48,487
[SPEAKER_03]: So, so I'm going to take on the task somehow of visiting all the schools.

1029
01:39:49,410 --> 01:40:00,334
[SPEAKER_03]: between the time these lights come in and their end of season on June 23rd and see what I can do for a light distribution through the public schools.

1030
01:40:00,814 --> 01:40:16,941
[SPEAKER_03]: That will of course inform how quickly I go through that 240 sets of lights and how quickly I'll wanna gear up again for a September, October distribution for the rest of the year, for the season.

1031
01:40:18,537 --> 01:40:21,583
[SPEAKER_03]: But these are turning out to be sort of wildly popular.

1032
01:40:22,926 --> 01:40:28,899
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, and I try to carry two kits with me daily when I ride around because I'll run into somebody.

1033
01:40:29,424 --> 01:40:36,026
[SPEAKER_03]: or a bunch of kids and give away one or two and then the other two will argue about why they don't have any.

1034
01:40:36,466 --> 01:40:39,266
[SPEAKER_03]: I have to bike home and get two more and go back, et cetera.

1035
01:40:39,346 --> 01:40:43,167
[SPEAKER_03]: And we have discussions about blinking and dinosaurs and all that.

1036
01:40:43,307 --> 01:40:46,488
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's really kind of been a lot of fun as well.

1037
01:40:47,008 --> 01:40:51,069
[SPEAKER_03]: So I'm glad I'm doing this because I think it's working out pretty well.

1038
01:40:53,367 --> 01:40:54,148
[SPEAKER_03]: So that's it.

1039
01:40:54,248 --> 01:40:56,488
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll order bike guides.

1040
01:40:56,649 --> 01:41:04,871
[SPEAKER_03]: I already have the other literature, except for the Medford Bicycling stickers, which Pat and I discussed briefly.

1041
01:41:04,991 --> 01:41:13,274
[SPEAKER_03]: And I don't know if, Pat, you think that we're going to get those, if we're going to tweak the design or not, whether that can

1042
01:41:14,392 --> 01:41:18,714
[SPEAKER_03]: be done and get another few hundred of those printed within the next few weeks.

1043
01:41:19,155 --> 01:41:26,258
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a kind of a nice thing to be kids to be able to put on the bike and identify that they are a cyclist in Medford.

1044
01:41:26,839 --> 01:41:34,863
[SPEAKER_03]: And also that the kid is provided by us in Medford and for distribution, you know, to us.

1045
01:41:34,963 --> 01:41:37,984
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a civic pride thing for protecting the kids.

1046
01:41:38,024 --> 01:41:39,065
[SPEAKER_03]: And the last question is,

1047
01:41:40,143 --> 01:41:44,246
[SPEAKER_03]: that I have, our topic is related to what Bruce made up last time.

1048
01:41:44,726 --> 01:41:52,692
[SPEAKER_03]: How do I contact adults in need of lesser means who might take advantage of this program?

1049
01:41:54,153 --> 01:41:59,557
[SPEAKER_03]: I've used the K in the acronym to mean kits, bike light kits.

1050
01:41:59,657 --> 01:42:04,141
[SPEAKER_03]: And now I've heard two people say bike lights for kids, which I think is pretty cool.

1051
01:42:04,641 --> 01:42:07,123
[SPEAKER_03]: But I don't know if we wanna limit it just to that.

1052
01:42:08,011 --> 01:42:10,852
[SPEAKER_03]: distribution or just initially?

1053
01:42:10,912 --> 01:42:14,352
[SPEAKER_14]: I think getting them to adults is great.

1054
01:42:14,472 --> 01:42:21,694
[SPEAKER_14]: And adults are more likely to be riding at night in the dark than kids because kids have bedtimes in the summer.

1055
01:42:22,034 --> 01:42:23,054
[SPEAKER_03]: But how do I do that?

1056
01:42:23,214 --> 01:42:31,216
[SPEAKER_03]: Who can I talk to who has something to do with housing or populations in need in Medford?

1057
01:42:32,736 --> 01:42:34,937
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know where to start with contacts there.

1058
01:42:36,068 --> 01:42:37,688
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm not that civically linked.

1059
01:42:38,308 --> 01:42:45,190
[SPEAKER_03]: So if you guys have any ideas on how to talk to people in those populations, I will bless you.

1060
01:42:45,650 --> 01:42:47,771
[SPEAKER_03]: I will try to pursue that during the summer.

1061
01:42:50,091 --> 01:42:55,132
[SPEAKER_10]: There's a food kitchen of Universalist church on High Street.

1062
01:42:55,152 --> 01:42:56,893
[SPEAKER_10]: I think it's last Thursday of every month.

1063
01:42:58,273 --> 01:42:58,953
[SPEAKER_10]: That might be a way.

1064
01:43:00,020 --> 01:43:02,521
[SPEAKER_03]: And there are people who might bike there as an example.

1065
01:43:04,102 --> 01:43:18,628
[SPEAKER_14]: I also wonder if there's a way to get in touch with like food service workers at restaurants who often are going home late at night and often are going home at times when the buses are not running that frequently.

1066
01:43:18,648 --> 01:43:27,292
[SPEAKER_14]: I think any place we can find a lot of adults who might be riding around

1067
01:43:28,413 --> 01:43:31,115
[SPEAKER_14]: at night or might be interested in riding around at night.

1068
01:43:31,816 --> 01:43:45,728
[SPEAKER_14]: Honestly, I don't even care if they have the means to buy their own bike lights, but if we can hand them this kit that has lights and some safety information and some information about biking in Medford and that encourages them to bike somewhere.

1069
01:43:47,357 --> 01:43:52,780
[SPEAKER_14]: so much, I don't really care who they are if that gets them out on the bike.

1070
01:43:54,021 --> 01:44:14,552
[SPEAKER_03]: Even though these are only four bucks a kit and half of it we have co-sponsors for only costing us two bucks a kit, I've been very strict about limiting it to residents, kids of Medford and maybe their parents, which I maybe shouldn't have had because there have been kids around who are clearly in need and they live in Arlington or Somerville.

1071
01:44:14,632 --> 01:44:16,493
[SPEAKER_03]: And I say, oh, sorry.

1072
01:44:16,953 --> 01:44:23,954
[SPEAKER_14]: Well, also somebody who's biking, somebody, you don't have to live in Medford to bike in Medford and we care about anybody who's biking in Medford.

1073
01:44:23,974 --> 01:44:25,395
[SPEAKER_14]: Yeah.

1074
01:44:26,335 --> 01:44:26,595
[SPEAKER_08]: Okay.

1075
01:44:26,895 --> 01:44:37,237
[SPEAKER_08]: I thought last month we talked about the Medford Housing Authority and them wanting some bike share stations, so I don't know who the head of the Medford Housing Authority is, but that might be another avenue.

1076
01:44:37,797 --> 01:44:40,498
[SPEAKER_03]: But they are known as the Medford Housing Authority?

1077
01:44:40,738 --> 01:44:41,758
[SPEAKER_03]: I believe so, yeah.

1078
01:44:42,418 --> 01:44:45,319
[SPEAKER_03]: All right, I'll start by digging there.

